   "text": "I read the structure as a head-to-tail macrocyclic peptide and wrote it out residue by residue. The residues are Asn, cyclohexylglycine (Chg), a 4-triazolyl-proline, 3-benzothienylalanine and Lys-NH2, closed by a succinyl linker.\n\n**Connectivity**\n- The Asn side chain is CH2C(=O)NH2. Its \u03b1-NH is acylated by a succinyl bridge, C(=O)CH2CH2C(=O), which joins to the Lys \u03b5-NH.\n- The Lys chain is NH(CH2)4 up to the \u03b1-carbon. The \u03b1-carbon carries a C-terminal C(=O)NH2, and its \u03b1-NH is acylated by the benzothienylalanine carbonyl.\n- The benzothienylalanine side chain is CH2 attached to benzothiophene at C3.\n- The benzothienylalanine \u03b1-NH is acylated by the proline carbonyl. The proline N is acylated by the Chg carbonyl, and the Chg NH closes the ring onto the Asn carbonyl.\n- Proline C4 carries a 1,2,3-triazol-1-yl group. Triazole C4 bears a 4-(thiophen-2-yl)phenyl group.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n\nI assigned each centre from its wedge or hash. All six centres come out as the L-configuration (S): Asn, Lys, benzothienylalanine, Pro C2, Chg, and the triazole-bearing Pro C4. For Pro C4 the triazole N is on a wedge, and I made that centre S by CIP priority.\n\nI checked the SMILES for valid ring closures and atom counts against the drawing.\n\nSMILES: NC(=O)C[C@@H]1NC(=O)CCC(=O)NCCCC[C@@H](C(N)=O)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc2csc3ccccc23)NC(=O)[C@@H]2C[C@H](n3cc(-c4ccc(-c5cccs5)cc4)nn3)CN2C(=O)[C@H](C2CCCCC2)NC1=O"
